A||N||4||maffthekat||Arrrghhhh: TPS problems||||8||
Z||000000||maffthekat||07-11-2001||03:57 AM||||Hi people,<BR>I had problems with my fiero idling rough as a dog and juddering during cruising so I checked the computer and it told me that the throttle position sensor (code 21) and coolant temp sensor were bad so I ordered new ones from the Fiero store and replaced the bogus bits.<BR>Had to remove the top of the throttle body to get the darned TPS off and took care to replace every hose and bolt.<BR>Reset the computer and started my baby up and checked again, it's cleared the coolant sensor code, but now it gives me a code 22 (throttle position sensor again)!!! <BR>Now when I turn the ignition on the check engine light is permanently on and if I push the accelerator pedal down too fast the car stumbles like crazy. By the way it's a 4 cylinder '84.<P>Has anybody got any ideas? Its damn annoying now I can't race those jammy Golf GTi drivers anymore!||||reg||8
Z||000001||maffthekat||07-11-2001||07:26 AM||||Has anyone got any ideas?? <BR>Is there anyone out there...?!||||reg||8
Z||000002||maffthekat||07-11-2001||08:07 AM||||Can someone answer me? I have put my faith in you fellow Fiero owners - you are my only hope....||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cozmo||07-11-2001||08:21 AM||||Sounds like it could be a number of things...<BR>First, check the wiring harness for damage between the sensors and the ECM. Also, verify all the grounds (earths, in England?) are connected between the engine, body and battery.<BR>If all this looks OK, it may be fuel related. Change the fuel filter. If you can, check the fuel pressure also.<BR>As an aside, I had an '87 DIS 4cyl. that did the same thing, it ended up being a faulty coil pack.<BR>Good luck, let us know what you find...<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||07-11-2001||08:26 AM||||Sorry we're so slow to respond... might have to do with the language barrier  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Anyway, at the risk of telling you what you already know, you might want to measure the voltages (with respect to ground/ earth) at each of the TPS's terminals, using a  voltmeter.<P>The output (center terminal, I think-?) should vary smoothly with throttle position, while the other terminals should read about 5v at one terminal, and zero at the other.<P>Please keep in mind, I'm not an expert on the 4-cylinder models.  However, one of the first things I would check is the MAP sensor.<P>FWIW, since you got code 21 (high TPS) and later code 22 (low TPS), I wonder if the 5v power supply in the computer isn't regulating  properly?  Just groping at this point...          ||||reg||12
